  "limitations": [
    "The DIGEST does not cover the EXECUTABLE an oracle or the driver names. Files under oracles.dir are covered byte for byte; the binary, interpreter, shared library, container image or PATH entry resolved at run time is NOT hashed into manifest_sha. The `executables` block records each resolved program's SHA-256 OUTSIDE the digest, with the `platform` it was taken on. On that platform a changed, missing or newly resolvable program is ORACLE_DRIFT (exit 4, design record.); on any other platform it is a WARNING that also lands in the verdict, because a build for another OS can never match . A program is reproducible from unchanged source only if it is built without a commit stamp. Only the program is fingerprinted — not its arguments, its interpreter, or anything it loads — and nothing short of signing makes a swap impossible.",
    "File modes are not covered. The executable bit does not survive a Windows checkout, so hashing it would fire a spurious ORACLE_DRIFT on a cross-platform clone.",
    "Built-in oracle thresholds are compiled in and reachable from no config key. builtin_options_fingerprint records them for review but is OUTSIDE the digest: including it would turn any release that adjusts a default into exit 4 on every downstream project at once. `synthesis verify-lock` reports a moved fingerprint as a warning, never as drift.",
    "The driver is covered by its COMMAND and by the driver profiles a run profile names . A driver profile no run profile names is not covered, because it is not the gate's workload and covering it would fire exit 4 on routine edits. The driver's PROGRAM is fingerprinted in the `executables` block as `driver.cmd`, outside the digest, so a rebuilt or swapped driver is a WARNING and not drift. Neither the workload a driver compiles in for a profile name nor the environment it inherits is covered: a driver that reads its workload from its own table or from environment variables can still be changed without moving this digest.",
    "The digest covers the USER-SUPPLIED synthesis.yaml with defaults NOT applied. A key you did not write contributes nothing, so upgrading thesis cannot move this digest by changing a compiled-in default."
  ],
